=== task: mcq medium
title: How slow is the simple method?
prompt: The simple method from the lesson does V rounds, and each round searches through the jobs and their prerequisite lists for a free one. On a graph with V jobs and E edges, about how much work can it take in the worst case?
* O(V × (V + E)) :: Right. Each of the V rounds may look at every job and every prerequisite.
- O(V + E) :: That's Kahn's algorithm, which keeps track of the free jobs instead of searching for one each round.
- O(V) :: Finding a free job alone may mean scanning many jobs, each round.
- O(2ⁿ) :: Nothing here tries every possible order; it builds one order directly.
hint: How many rounds are there?
hint: How much can one round's search cost?
right: V rounds of up to O(V + E) work each: O(V × (V + E)). Kahn's algorithm, in the next lesson, cuts it to O(V + E) by counting unfinished prerequisites and queuing jobs as they become free.
wrong: There's one round per job, and each round may scan all the jobs and their prerequisites again, so the total is V times O(V + E).

